# A full harness

A workbook unit can be a complete, self-contained system. Here's one file with an
**agent** that works tasks, a **flow** that drives it over the backlog, the tasks it
operates on (from [[Plan]]), and a **test** that gates the whole thing.

agent :dispatcher do
  model "minimax/minimax-m3"

  system """
  You manage the sales backlog. Claim the highest-leverage unclaimed task, do it
  with your tools, close it. Never touch a task you don't own.
  """

  tools [Claims.claim/1, Claims.finish/1, Analyst.draft_outreach/1]

  # route a task to the specialist by the unit it references
  def route(%Task{ref: :analyst}), do: :analyst
  def route(%Task{ref: _}),        do: :dispatcher
end

The flow pulls open tasks, claims each under its fence, runs the specialist, and
closes it. Two claims in one `parallel` touch different fences, so they never contend.

server flow :work_board do
  step :pull, do: query(:tasks, where: &(&1.status == :todo))

  step :assign, after: :pull do
    parallel for task <- result(:pull) do
      who = dispatcher().route(task)
      with {:ok, :claimed} <- claims().claim(task.title, who),
           {:ok, _}        <- run(who, task) do
        claims().finish(task.title, who)
      end
    end
  end
end

test :work_board do
  test "a claimed task can't be claimed twice" do
    {:ok, :claimed} = Claims.claim("Forecast next-quarter pipe", :ada)
    assert {:error, {:already_claimed, :ada}} = Claims.claim("Forecast next-quarter pipe", :rex)
  end
end

Agent, flow, tasks, test — one file, a working system. A red test ships no bundle.
